div.p:
Thirdly, the Second World War spanned two decades. That this fact mangled some of the data this is suggested by a leaflet in the ‘no consensus’ sample containing patterns for a man’s pullover and jumper Designed in Consultation with the Admiralty (for those serving in the navy), which was dated 1910s by one volunteer and 1930s and 1940s by two each. Since 4/5 recognised, correctly, that the leaflet had been printed during the Second World War, a consensus had in fact been reached. It failed to appear in the data however, because two selected the decade in which the conflict began and two, the decade in which it ended. Flawed as they are, the data collected by workflow 1 suggest that there may be other cases of this in the ‘no consensus’ group. As Table 1 shows, volunteers agreed that the 1940s was the most prolific decade within the dataset. This may reflect the importance of knitting during the wartime and post-war years, which might have resulted in a larger number of patterns being saved;

 During the war, the handicraft became a form of national service, with knitters being encouraged to make garments and other comforts for service personnel, using wool that, in some circumstances, was available off ration . Leaflets for service knitwear may, therefore, have been kept by those who wanted to make or remake items to ensure the comfort of those posted in difficult and dangerous conditions. Closer to home, knitting was a means of coping with shortages of clothing and fuel; as one Make Do and Mend booklet explained, a variety of warm garments, including underwear and coat linings, could be made using wool recycled from unwanted knitwear . That the handicraft retained this function after the war, clothing being rationed until 1949 (), might explain why leaflets that were produced after the war ended in 1945 were kept. Finally, knitting was framed throughout the war as a means of self-care and escapism, and a connection with peacetime life .

besides which, more recent collectors may have thought wartime patterns to have greater historic value, resulting in a higher volume of patterns from the 1940s in the KCG’s Collection. It is also possible, however, that the ‘no consensus’ group contains further wartime patterns, on whose publication decade volunteers agreed in effect but disagreed in the data.
